<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="AZD: Work with pull requests" id="Work-with-Azure-DevOps-pull-requests">

    <primary-label ref="azd"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Open: <ui-path>View | Tool Windows | AZD Pull Requests</ui-path> or <ui-path>Git | AZD | Show AZD Pull Requests</ui-path></p>
        <p>Requires: a project with an Azure DevOps Git remote and a signed-in AZD account</p>
        <p>Preferences: <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | Preferences</ui-path></p>
    </tldr>

    <link-summary>Open the AZD Pull Requests tool window, then find, filter, sort and open Azure DevOps pull requests.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Find, filter and open Azure DevOps pull requests in the AZD Pull Requests tool window.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>Browse Azure DevOps pull requests in JetBrains IDEs with AZD: filters, quick filters, custom
        filters, sorting, drafts and a pull request metrics dashboard.</web-summary>

    <p>
        The <control>AZD Pull Requests</control> tool window lists the pull requests of the Azure DevOps repository that your project is cloned from.
        From the list, you open a pull request, create a new one and track your team's review activity.
    </p>
    <!-- planned-image src="azd_pr_overview.png" alt="AZD Pull Requests tool window next to a pull request diff with an open comment thread" -->
    <p>
        Tasks for a single pull request have their own topics:
    </p>
    <list>
        <li><a href="Azd-Create-Pull-Request.topic"/></li>
        <li><a href="Pull-Request-Details.topic"/></li>
        <li><a href="Azd-PR-Comments.topic"/></li>
        <li><a href="Azd-PR-Complete.topic"/></li>
    </list>

    <chapter title="Open the tool window" id="open-tool-window">
        <p>
            The tool window is available when the project has a Git remote that points to Azure DevOps Services or Azure DevOps Server.
            To sign in, see <a href="Azd-Installation.topic" anchor="connect"/>.
        </p>
        <procedure title="Open the AZD Pull Requests tool window" id="open-pr-tool-window" type="choices">
            <step>
                Select <ui-path>View | Tool Windows | AZD Pull Requests</ui-path>.
            </step>
            <step>
                In the main menu, select <ui-path>Git | AZD | Show AZD Pull Requests</ui-path>.
                <!-- planned-image src="azd_pr_open_menu.png" alt="Git menu with the AZD submenu open and Show AZD Pull Requests highlighted" -->
            </step>
            <step>
                Click the <control>AZD Pull Requests</control> icon on the tool window bar.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            If AZD cannot choose the repository and the account on its own, for example because the project has more than one Azure DevOps repository,
            the tool window asks you to select them.
            To select them, or to switch later with <control>Change Repository or Account…</control>, see <a href="Azd-Installation.topic" anchor="azd-select-repository"/>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="View pull requests" id="view-pull-requests">
        <p>
            The <control>Pull Requests</control> tab lists the pull requests of the repository, newest first.
            When you open the list in a project for the first time, it shows only your own active pull requests (the <control>Includes my changes</control> quick filter).
            To see all active pull requests, select the <control>Active</control> quick filter; see <a anchor="quick-filters"/>.
            AZD remembers the last filter for the project.
        </p>
        <p>
            Each row shows the title, the pull request number, the creation date and author, labels and reviewer avatars.
        </p>
        <!-- planned-image src="azd_pr_list.png" alt="Pull request list with filters, a DRAFT badge, labels and reviewer avatars" -->
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="DRAFT, MERGED, ABANDONED">
                The state badge of a pull request that is not active.
            </def>
            <def title="Merge conflict icon">
                The tooltip <control>Pull request can't be merged</control> means that the source branch conflicts with the target branch.
            </def>
            <def title="New commits icon">
                The tooltip <control>New commits since your review</control> means that commits were pushed after you last voted or marked every file as viewed.
                AZD keeps track of these reviews for 30 days.
            </def>
        </deflist>
        <p>
            Double-click a pull request, or select it and press <shortcut>Enter</shortcut>, to open its details in a new tab of the tool window.
            Right-click a pull request for <control>Show Pull Request</control>, <control>Open Pull Request in Browser</control>,
            <control>Copy Pull Request URL</control> and <control>Refresh</control>.
        </p>
        <p>
            By default, the list hides draft pull requests.
            To show them, click <control>Include Drafts</control> on the list toolbar.
            To hide them again, click <control>Exclude Drafts</control>.
            AZD remembers the choice for the project.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Filter and search" id="filter-pull-requests">
        <p>
            Type in the search field to filter the list as you type.
            AZD matches the text against the title, the description and the number of each pull request.
            Use the filter buttons under the search field to narrow the list further:
        </p>
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="State">
                <control>All</control>, <control>Active</control>, <control>Merged</control> or <control>Abandoned</control>.
            </def>
            <def title="Author, Reviewer">
                A person in the organization.
                Type at least 2 characters to search for a user.
            </def>
            <def title="Source Branch, Target Branch">
                A branch of the repository.
            </def>
        </deflist>
        <p>
            If no pull request matches, click <control>Clear filters</control> in the empty list.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Quick filters" id="quick-filters">
        <p>
            Click the filter icon in the search field to open <control>Quick Filters</control>.
            Every built-in quick filter except <control>Abandoned</control> shows active pull requests only.
        </p>
        <!-- planned-image src="azd_pr_quick_filters.png" alt="Quick Filters popup listing the built-in filters and one custom filter" -->
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Active">All active pull requests.</def>
            <def title="Includes my changes">Pull requests that you created.</def>
            <def title="I am a reviewer">Pull requests where you are a reviewer.</def>
            <def title="Abandoned">Abandoned pull requests.</def>
            <def title="Waiting for Author">Pull requests where you voted <control>Wait For Author</control>.</def>
            <def title="I Reviewed">Pull requests that you approved, with or without suggestions.</def>
            <def title="Awaiting My Vote">Pull requests where you are a reviewer and have not voted yet.</def>
            <def title="New Changes Since Review">Pull requests with commits pushed after your last review.</def>
        </deflist>
        <p>
            Your custom quick filters appear in the same popup.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Custom quick filters" id="custom-quick-filters">
        <p>
            A custom quick filter saves a combination of criteria under a name, for example "Needs my vote on main".
        </p>
        <procedure title="Create a custom quick filter" id="create-custom-quick-filter">
            <step>
                In the title bar of the <control>AZD Pull Requests</control> tool window, click <control>Custom Quick Filters</control> (the gear icon).
                You can also right-click the list and select <control>Custom Quick Filters</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                In the <control>Custom Quick Filters</control> dialog, click <control>+</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                In the <control>Add Custom Quick Filter</control> dialog, enter a <control>Name</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Under <control>Filter Criteria</control>, set any of <control>State</control>, <control>Sort</control>,
                <control>Author</control>, <control>Reviewer</control>, <control>Source Branch</control> and <control>Target Branch</control>.
                <!-- planned-image src="azd_pr_custom_quick_filter.png" alt="Add Custom Quick Filter dialog with a name, the Active state and a reviewer selected" -->
            </step>
            <step>
                Click <control>OK</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            To change or delete a custom quick filter, open the <control>Custom Quick Filters</control> dialog, select the filter and click the edit button or <control>-</control>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Sort the list" id="sort-pull-requests">
        <p>
            Click the sort icon on the list toolbar and select a <control>Sort by</control> option:
            <control>Default (newest first)</control>, <control>Created Date</control> or <control>Title</control>.
            Select <control>Descending</control> to reverse the order.
            AZD remembers the sort order for each project.
        </p>
        <!-- planned-image src="azd_pr_sort.png" alt="Sort by popup for pull requests with Created Date selected" -->
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Keep the list up to date" id="auto-refresh">
        <p>
            To reload the list, right-click it and select <control>Refresh</control>, or press the shortcut assigned to <control>Refresh</control> (see <ui-path>Settings | Keymap</ui-path>).
        </p>
        <p>
            To refresh the list and open pull requests automatically, select <control>Auto-refresh pull requests</control> and set <control>Interval in seconds</control>
            in <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | Preferences</ui-path>.
            AZD refreshes the list only while its tab is shown.
            It refreshes an open pull request only while its details tab is shown and has the focus.
            For all options on this page, see <a href="Azd-Settings-Configuration.topic" anchor="general-configuration"/>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="PR metrics" id="pr-metrics">
        <p id="azd_pr_metrics_usage">
            The <control>PR Metrics</control> dashboard summarizes pull request activity in the repository.
            It needs the IDE's built-in browser (JCEF).
        </p>
        <procedure title="Open the PR metrics dashboard" id="open-pr-metrics">
            <step>
                In the title bar of the <control>AZD Pull Requests</control> tool window, click <control>PR Metrics</control> (the chart icon).
            </step>
            <step>
                In the <control>PR Metrics</control> editor tab, use the toolbar to set the <control>Time range</control>
                and to filter by <control>Author</control>, <control>Reviewer</control> and <control>Target branch</control>.
                The time range goes from <control>Last 7 days</control> to <control>Last year</control>; the default is <control>Last 90 days</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>The dashboard shows:</p>
        <list>
            <li>Summary cards: <control>Total PRs</control>, <control>Active</control>, <control>Merged</control>, <control>Avg Cycle Time</control> and <control>Merges / Week</control>.</li>
            <li><control>PR Cycle Time</control>: the time from creation to completion of each merged pull request.</li>
            <li><control>Status Distribution</control> and <control>Review Votes</control>.</li>
            <li>The number of pull requests merged per day, week or month, depending on the time range.</li>
            <li><control>Top Authors</control> and <control>Top Reviewers</control>.</li>
        </list>
        <p>
            Click the maximize button of a widget to enlarge it.
            For pipeline metrics and agent pools, see <a href="Azd-Pipeline-Metrics.topic"/>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Create a pull request" id="create-pull-request">
        <p>
            Click <control>Create Pull Request…</control> (<control>+</control>) in the tool window title bar.
            For all the ways to start and the fields of the new pull request tab, see <a href="Azd-Create-Pull-Request.topic"/>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

